      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">On October 2, 2026, the G7 leaders agreed to a coordinated release through the International Energy Agency of 100 million barrels over four months, beginning immediately, with a substantial diesel share in the first 20 days and no number for that diesel. They will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible, and they will refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries. They set the release "taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled," and did not say how much of the 100 million is new beyond the March commitments.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">U.S. retail diesel on September 28 was above its March 2 price. The G7 leaders said the measures are meant to "shield households and businesses from price shocks". Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said American farmers, truckers, and businesses should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage. EU diesel supply remains stable for the time being, but prices remain high. Prices have rocketed in recent months because of the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia's war in Ukraine.<Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-28,6.382" /><Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-03-02,3.897" /><Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="Prices have rocketed in recent months because of the disruption in global markets caused by the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia’s ongoing war in Ukraine." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="shield households and businesses from price shocks" /><Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="American farmers, truckers, and businesses should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage" /><Cite s="ec-taskforce-2026-10-02" passage="EU diesel supply remains stable for the time being, but prices remain high" /></p>
        
        <p data-layer="narrative">France puts the ceiling at up to 100 million barrels within four months, covering refined products, diesel, and crude.<Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="produits raffinés, notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut, jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">By October 2, members had released most of what they pledged in March, and the United States was still carrying out the share it announced then. On September 29 the United States offered an exchange of crude, and the companies that take the barrels will return them later with additional premium barrels. On September 30 President Donald Trump was still thinking about banning diesel exports. On October 1 Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available. On October 2 the leaders agreed to a coordinated release of 100 million barrels over four months, with a substantial diesel share in the first 20 days. Hours later President Trump said he will not authorize an export ban on diesel. The seven countries committed to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between themselves. The March release was still being carried out, the October statement set a four-month timetable with an early diesel share, and the United States dropped the export-ban threat.<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="participating companies will return the 40 million borrowed barrels with additional premium barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="continuing DOE’s execution of the 172-million-barrel release previously announced by President Trump in coordination with the International Energy Agency (IEA) member nations’ overall 400-million-barrel commitment" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage={`still "thinking about" banning diesel exports`} /><Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="Our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available" /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel" /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, hours after G7 countries agreed to release diesel and crude oil from their emergency reserves." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>March pledge and what followed</strong><span>The agency members' March pledge, the barrels released by October 2, and the United States share and exchange.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Release", "Barrels"]} rows={volumeRows} />
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Japan has no plans for a further release at this stage. Chief Cabinet Secretary Minoru Kihara said, "Japan has been releasing oil from reserves for some time now and there are no plans for a further release at this stage." In March Japan said it would release about 80 million barrels of oil from its reserves as part of the coordinated agreement.<Cite s="reuters-japan-no-release-2026-10-05" passage="Japanese Chief Cabinet Secretary Minoru Kihara" /><Cite s="reuters-japan-no-release-2026-10-05" passage="Japan has been releasing oil from reserves for some time now and there are no plans for a further release at this stage" /><Cite s="reuters-japan-no-release-2026-10-05" passage="In March, Japan said it would release about 80 million barrels of oil from its reserves as part of the coordinated agreement." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>No account of the October 2 meeting shows a barrel of the October release delivered.</p>
        
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">A barrel counted as crude is not the diesel a pump sells. In March the overall release of emergency stocks was to consist largely of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions were to be primarily refined products. That split between crude and refined products remained subject to change. In October the countries will coordinate maintenance schedules across their refineries to prevent simultaneous shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization where feasible, and they encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacity to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel. Net exports of diesel and gasoil from the Gulf countries averaged 390,000 barrels a day in August, just over a quarter of levels before the war in the Middle East, and exports from the Gulf and Russia together were 1.6 million barrels a day lower than in February. Volumes moving through Hormuz, the Strait of Hormuz where the G7 leaders call for the restoration of navigational rights, and through the Yanbu canal in the Red Sea have been rising in recent days. On September 30, Energy Secretary Chris Wright said diesel supplies are "tight" because conflicts involving Russia and the Middle East have cut exports, and that American refiners are running at record highs.<Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="averaged 390 kb/d in August, just over a quarter of pre-war levels" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="Combined, net exports of diesel/gasoil exports from the Gulf and Russia in August were 1.6 mb/d lower than in February" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage={`diesel supplies are "tight" because of export loss related to conflicts in Russia and the Middle East`} /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Les volumes acheminés transitant par Ormuz et dans le canal de Yanbu en Mer Rouge augmentent ces derniers jours" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage="American refiners are running at record highs" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The G7 leaders' statement says the group will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and "temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible", and "We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel".<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel" /></p>
          <p>The same statement says the release takes into account "commitments that have already been fulfilled," asks the International Energy Agency to monitor the March 2026 commitments, and says the group will meet again "to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary." It also says the members "reaffirm our commitment to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries," and that a follow-up report "should be delivered before 20 days." It says the report should "include actionable recommendations to enhance future responses, including stocks replenishment." It does not say 20 days from when, or who delivers the report. It calls for the immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="monitor the immediate and full implementation of the March 2026 commitments" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="A follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days, include actionable recommendations to enhance future responses, including stocks replenishment." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The French presidency published the same English statement, including the {release.barrels} barrel release over {release.months} and the diesel release "within the first 20 days."<Cite s="elysee-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On March 11, Chris Wright said 32 member nations agreed to a "coordinated release of 400 million barrels," and that the United States would "release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, beginning next week," over "approximately 120 days." He said the United States had arranged to replace the draw with "approximately 200 million barrels within the next year."<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, beginning next week" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-communique-2026-03-11" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The March 11 G7 presidency communique says leaders welcomed the International Energy Agency announcement, prepared at the G7's initiative, "de libérer jusqu’à 400 millions de barils de réserves stratégiques", to release up to 400 million barrels of strategic reserves.<Cite s="elysee-g7-communique-2026-03-11" passage="de libérer jusqu’à 400 millions de barils de réserves stratégiques" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The agency's account of the same meeting lists President Donald Trump, Chancellor Friedrich Merz, Prime Minister Mark Carney, Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni, President Ursula von der Leyen, UK Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband, and Japanese Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs Kazuya Endo.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="The meeting was attended by President Donald Trump of the United States, Chancellor Friedrich Merz of Germany, Prime Minister Mark Carney of Canada, Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni of Italy, President Ursula von der Leyen of the European Commission, UK Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband and Japanese Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs Kazuya Endo." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Independent reported that Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband would stand in for Andy Burnham during the talks, as the Prime Minister attended his father's funeral.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband will stand in for Andy Burnham during urgent G7 talks regarding global energy markets, as the Prime Minister attends his father’s funeral." /></p>
          <p>The same article says fuel prices hit £2 a litre for the first time.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="fuel prices hit £2 a litre for the first time" /></p>
          <p>It says the previous record high was 199.09p, set in June 2022.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="The previous record high was 199.09p, set in June 2022" /></p>
          <p>The same article says prices have rocketed because of the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia's ongoing war in Ukraine.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="Prices have rocketed in recent months because of the disruption in global markets caused by the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia’s ongoing war in Ukraine." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The French presidency's readout says the G7 leaders decided unanimously. It says a letter from the president of the Republic to the president of the European Commission concerned running refineries at full capacity, and that first measures had already been decided at European level.<Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Ils ont décidé, à l'unanimité :" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="la lettre que le Président de la République avait envoyée à la Présidente de la Commission européenne" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Les volumes acheminés transitant par Ormuz et dans le canal de Yanbu en Mer Rouge augmentent ces derniers jours" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The International Energy Agency's March 19 contributions page describes the 11 March agreement as one "to make 400 million barrels of oil available to the market". It says the overall release "will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products." It calls the action "the sixth time" members have acted together since the agency "was created in 1974," after actions "in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022," and says the war in the Middle East is creating "the largest supply disruption in the history of the global oil market." Executive Director Fatih Birol's remarks are named on that page. The contributions workbook's total line, 426.03075080054771, is the sum of the listed contributions, in the column marked (mb). That sum is not a second release and not barrels delivered.<Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="to make 400 million barrels of oil available to the market" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="This is the sixth time that IEA Member countries have taken emergency collective action" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="Previous collective actions were taken in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022." /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="The war in the Middle East is creating the largest supply disruption in the history of the global oil market" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-workbook-2026-03-19" passage="Total IEA,426.03075080054771" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof">
          <summary>English statement and French readout</summary>
          <DataTable columns={["Record", "Wording of the 100 million barrels"]} rows={statementRows} />
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>A diesel release, and no number for it</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">The G7 text commits the leaders to a coordinated release of 100 million barrels over four months, with a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days, and it names no diesel amount and no country that will supply the barrels. Before that agreement the United States had demanded 100 million barrels of diesel from large European countries within 20 days. European Union countries that day discussed a French proposal for 50 million barrels of diesel from Europe and 50 million barrels of crude oil from International Energy Agency members. The demand and the proposal were positions before the agreement. The Wall Street Journal's lede said the countries would "release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks". France's account of the meeting covers refined products, diesel, and crude. President Donald Trump posted that Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil and that the process will begin immediately. On October 5 the White House said he had negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves within the next four months. None of the White House claim, the joint text, or the French readout is a record of barrels delivered.<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="In October 2026, President Trump successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves within the next four months." /><Cite s="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" passage="Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately." /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="The US had demanded large European countries including France and Germany release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="European Union countries discussed a French proposal on Friday to release diesel stocks" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="European countries would release 50 million barrels of diesel and International Energy Agency members would release 50 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="wsj-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="produits raffinés, notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut, jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The words "100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States" are an X post's wording, attributed to Agriculture Secretary Brooke Rollins. In the published excerpts of her interview she says the administration would do everything it could in the short term.<Cite s="daugherty-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States, per Sec. Brooke Rollins" /><Cite s="scripps-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="For the short term, we're going to do everything we can" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">A later turn of the interview takes up a diesel export ban. Asked about that ban, she said she is the agriculture secretary, not energy, and is not in those conversations. In the closed captions she says, "100 million barrels that will be headed this way very, very soon. fact, I think they’re moving out right now." She does not call the barrels diesel in that passage. That post turns her I think into a flat claim and adds diesel.<Cite s="scripps-rollins-interview-captions-2026-10-02" passage="100 million barrels that will be headed this way very, very soon. fact, I think they’re moving out right now." /><Cite s="scripps-rollins-interview-captions-2026-10-02" passage="I’m the secretary of agriculture, not energy, and I’m I’m certainly not in any of these conversations." /><Cite s="daugherty-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States, per Sec. Brooke Rollins" /></p>

            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Oil reserve decisions</strong><span>March to October 2026.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={chronology} />
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Diesel and crude</strong><span>Weekly U.S. retail diesel, Brent (the Europe crude price), and WTI (the U.S. crude price at Cushing).</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Series", "Observation", "Dollars"]} rows={priceRows} />
        </figure>
        <SourcedBlock source="gasdesw-2026-10-03" kind="computed" detail>
          <p>Diesel is {prices.dieselFromMarch} dollars a gallon, or {prices.dieselFromMarchPercent} percent, above March 2. Brent, the Europe crude price, is {prices.brentFromFebruary} dollars a barrel above February 27. WTI, the U.S. crude price at Cushing, is {prices.wtiFromFebruary} dollars above February 27.<Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-03-02,3.897" /><Cite s="dcoilbrenteu-2026-10-03" passage="2026-02-27,71.32" /><Cite s="dcoilwtico-2026-10-03" passage="2026-02-27,66.96" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>How far prices have moved</strong><span>The change in weekly diesel since September 21 and since March 2, and the change in Brent and WTI since February 27.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Comparison", "Change", "Unit"]} rows={changeRows} />
        </figure>
        <SourcedBlock source="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 29 the Energy Department issued a request for an "exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, continuing its execution of the 172 million barrel release announced in March. Participating companies return the borrowed barrels later with extra barrels. Secretary Chris Wright said several European member countries "have released only a fraction" of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged. The department said five earlier solicitations "collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels," with a "25 percent premium in returned barrels." Bids are due "October 6, 2026." Deliveries under those awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="continuing DOE’s execution of the 172-million-barrel release previously announced by President Trump in coordination with the International Energy Agency (IEA) member nations’ overall 400-million-barrel commitment" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="participating companies will return the 40 million borrowed barrels with additional premium barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="several European member countries have released only a fraction" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="several European member countries have released only a fraction of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="Deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December 2026" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="bessent-post-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On October 1, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent wrote: "Our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available."<Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="Our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" kind="record" detail>
          <p>U.S. stocks of distillate fuel oil were {distillate.sep25} thousand barrels in the week of September 25, down from {distillate.sep18} thousand the week before.<Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="Stocks of Distillate Fuel Oil" /><Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="(Thousand Barrels)" /><Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="08/21/26 08/28/26 09/04/26 09/11/26 09/18/26 09/25/26 View History U.S. 103,391 104,187 106,274 107,859 107,431 105,180" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The German government's energy FAQ says the 32 IEA members agreed in March to release "400 Millionen Barrel (gut 54 Millionen Tonnen)," and that "Deutschland hat die Freigabe am 18. März erteilt." It says Germany's emergency reserve "für etwa 90 Tage umfasst insgesamt etwa 20 Millionen Tonnen," and that the IEA had asked Germany to release "2,6 Millionen Tonnen."<Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Deutschlands Notfallreserve für etwa 90 Tage umfasst insgesamt etwa 20 Millionen Tonnen" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Die IEA hatte Deutschland gebeten, 2,6 Millionen Tonnen aus seiner Reserve freizugeben." /></p>
          <p>The same FAQ says market participants have since called up a first tranche of the amount set for release, and that the market did not consider a further release necessary.<Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Eine erste Tranche dieser für die Freigabe vorgesehenen Mengen wurde mittlerweile von den Marktteilnehmern abgerufen" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Derzeit wird vom Markt keine weitere Freigabe für erforderlich gehalten." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="welt-g7-welcome-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Welt reported on Friday evening that the federal government welcomed the release of 100 million barrels of oil and products such as diesel.<Cite s="welt-g7-welcome-2026-10-02" passage="Auch die Bundesregierung hat den G-7-Beschluss zur Freigabe von 100 Millionen Barrel Öl und Ölprodukten wie Diesel als wichtiges Zeichen für die Handlungsfähigkeit und Geschlossenheit der Gruppe begrüßt." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="interfax-diesel-ban-2026-09-30" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Interfax reported on September 30 that the Russian government extended the ban on exports of diesel fuel for producers until October 31.<Cite s="interfax-diesel-ban-2026-09-30" passage="The Russian government has extended the ban on exports of diesel fuel, ship fuel and gasoil for producers, which was set to expire today, until October 31" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-novak-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On October 2 Reuters reported that Russia would consider a partial lifting of diesel export restrictions in case of overproduction.<Cite s="reuters-novak-2026-10-02" passage="Russia said on Friday it would consider a partial lifting of diesel export restrictions in case of overproduction" /></p>
          <p>The same account says Deputy Prime Minister Alexander Novak said the domestic diesel market is currently balanced.<Cite s="reuters-novak-2026-10-02" passage="the domestic diesel market is currently balanced" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Reuters reported that the amount of oil in the reserve had sunk to less than 284 million barrels, the lowest level since 1982.<Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="The amount of oil in US SPR has sunk to less than 284 million barrels, the lowest level since 1982." /></p>
          <p>The same article says US law prohibits the president from ordering routine, small draw downs if the SPR falls below 252.4 million barrels.<Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="US law prohibits the president from ordering routine, small draw downs if the SPR falls below 252.4 million barrels." /></p>
          <p>It says an earlier offer drew agreement to borrow about 500,000 barrels.<Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="companies only agreed to borrow about 500,000 barrels" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>

      </section>

      <section id="export-ban">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The export ban</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The United States has asked the European Union to release 120 million barrels of diesel in the next six months, according to an October 1 account. One European diplomat called the White House’s demands for a release, set against the threat of a U.S. export ban, “blackmail.”<Cite s="reuters-diesel-demand-2026-10-01" passage="A second source, based in a European capital, said that the US has asked the EU to release 120 million barrels of diesel in the next six months." /><Cite s="politico-diesel-pressure-2026-10-02" passage="One European diplomat characterized the White House’s demands for a release against the threat of a U.S. export ban explicitly as “blackmail,”" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On October 2 President Trump said he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, adding, "It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing." President Macron said Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel.<Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel" /><Cite s="politico-no-ban-2026-10-02" passage="It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing" /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="Mr Macron said Mr Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel" /></p>
        
        <p data-layer="narrative">The European Commission rejected a diesel ban. Spokeswoman Anna-Kaisa Itkonen said, "We fully reject any ban on diesel."<Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="We fully reject any ban on diesel." /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Ursula von der Leyen, European Commission president" when="October 2, 2026" where="X post, via RTÉ" label="RTÉ">
          <p>We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies and the continued solidarity between partners. We support an IEA-coordinated release of fuel stocks.<Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies" /><Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies and the continued solidarity between partners. We support an IEA-coordinated release of fuel stocks" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <SourcedBlock source="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 16, 2026, Mr. Burchett and Mr. Fuller introduced H.R. 10422, "To prohibit the export of diesel fuel from the United States during certain periods." The bill tells the Secretary of Commerce to prohibit diesel exports for a period that begins on the fourteenth consecutive day the average retail price is greater than $5 a gallon, and ends on the first day after thirty consecutive days on which that price is less than $4.50 a gallon. The text refers the bill to the Committee on Foreign Affairs.<Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="To prohibit the export of diesel fuel from the United States during" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="Secretary of Commerce shall prohibit the export of diesel fuel" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="begins on the date that is the fourteenth consecutive" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="greater than $5 per gallon" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="thirtieth consecutive day on which" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="less than $4.50 per gallon" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="which was referred to the Committee on Foreign Affairs" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="hr10423-diesel-export-2026-09-16" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The same day, the same sponsors introduced H.R. 10423. It says "the export of diesel fuel shall be prohibited" from enactment through "December 31, 2026." It too was referred to the Committee on Foreign Affairs.<Cite s="hr10423-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="the export of diesel fuel shall be prohibited during the period beginning on the date of the enactment of this subsection and ending on the date December 31, 2026." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="politico-diesel-pressure-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Politico reported that one European diplomat characterized the demand, set against the threat of a U.S. export ban, as blackmail.<Cite s="politico-diesel-pressure-2026-10-02" passage="One European diplomat characterized the White House’s demands for a release against the threat of a U.S. export ban explicitly as “blackmail,”" /><Cite s="reuters-diesel-demand-2026-10-01" passage="A second source, based in a European capital, said that the US has asked the EU to release 120 million barrels of diesel in the next six months." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="politico-no-ban-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Politico reported the president adding, "It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing".<Cite s="politico-no-ban-2026-10-02" passage="It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The National reported that Mr Macron said Mr Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel.<Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="Mr Macron said Mr Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>An RBC note says several leading Senate Republicans have publicly called for a diesel export ban.<Cite s="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" passage="several leading Senate Republicans have publicly called for a diesel export ban" /></p>
          <p>The same note quotes the president on September 22: "I've said let's not send out the diesel."<Cite s="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" passage="I've said let's not send out the diesel" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="blas-export-letter-2026-09-23" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 23 Javier Blas wrote that industry groups had sent a joint letter to President Trump urging him against limits on diesel exports.<Cite s="blas-export-letter-2026-09-23" passage="have sent a joint letter to President Trump urging him against limits of diesels exports." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="brownfield-dyed-diesel-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Brownfield reported that Ohio is implementing a 90-day state gas and diesel tax suspension.<Cite s="brownfield-dyed-diesel-2026-10-02" passage="Ohio is implementing a 90-day state gas and diesel tax suspension." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Xinhua quoted the spokesperson: "It would undermine our trust in the United States as a reliable partner."<Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="It would undermine our trust in the United States as a reliable partner." /></p>
          <p>The same account quotes the Commission president: "Our citizens need and deserve affordable energy."<Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="Our citizens need and deserve affordable energy" /></p>
          <p>It says that since late February, gas prices in Europe have risen by 140 percent and diesel prices have doubled.<Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="since late February, gas prices in Europe have risen by 140 percent and diesel prices have doubled" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="scripps-rollins-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Scripps quoted Agriculture Secretary Brooke Rollins: "We are in the middle of harvest season."<Cite s="scripps-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="We are in the middle of harvest season" /></p>
          <p>An X post the same day attributed to her a claim that 100 million barrels of diesel were en route to the United States. In the closed captions she says 100 million barrels will be headed this way very soon and that she thinks they are moving out right now, and she does not call them diesel in that passage. That post turns her I think into a flat claim and adds diesel. Asked about a diesel export ban, she said she is the agriculture secretary, not energy, and is not in those conversations.<Cite s="daugherty-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States, per Sec. Brooke Rollins" /><Cite s="scripps-rollins-interview-captions-2026-10-02" passage="100 million barrels that will be headed this way very, very soon. fact, I think they’re moving out right now." /><Cite s="scripps-rollins-interview-captions-2026-10-02" passage="I’m the secretary of agriculture, not energy, and I’m I’m certainly not in any of these conversations." /></p>
          <p>She also said, "For the short term, we're going to do everything we can."<Cite s="scripps-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="For the short term, we're going to do everything we can" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What comes next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The leaders will meet again through the International Energy Agency in the coming days to discuss more diesel releases if needed. They invite the agency to monitor the impact of the measures and whether the commitments are carried out. A follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days and should "include actionable recommendations to enhance future responses, including stocks replenishment." On October 5 President Trump signed an order saying restricted global diesel supply has led to rising prices and telling the Treasury secretary to defer the excise tax incurred from October 5 through December 31, 2026, if he determines the relief is authorized. Within five days the secretary is to direct the Internal Revenue Service to announce it will not impose a penalty when dyed diesel is used on the highway in that period. The White House says the order allows off-road dyed diesel for highway use. The measure is a tax and penalty step on U.S. fuel, not a reserve delivery. How much of the new release would come from what remains from the 400 million barrels of oil that the 32 countries that are members of the International Energy Agency agreed to release in March is uncertain.<Cite s="reuters-japan-no-release-2026-10-05" passage="It was uncertain how much of the new release would come from what remains from the 400 million barrels of oil that the 32 countries that are members of the International Energy Agency agreed to release in March to mitigate disruptions from the US-Israeli war on Iran." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will convene in the context of the IEA in the coming days to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="A follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="include actionable recommendations to enhance future responses, including stocks replenishment" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="Restricted global diesel supply has led to rising prices" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="If the Secretary makes those determinations, the Secretary shall, to the extent authorized by law, defer payment by those taxpayers of the taxes imposed by 26 U.S.C. 4041(a)(1)(A) or by 26 U.S.C. 4041(b)(1)(B) and that are incurred during the period of October 5, 2026, through December 31, 2026." /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="to announce that it will not impose a penalty" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="when dyed diesel fuel is sold for use or used on the highway during the period of October 5, 2026, through December 31, 2026" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="Today, President Donald J. Trump signed an Executive Order to temporarily allow off-road “dyed” diesel for highway use and defer the applicable Federal excise tax" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Participating companies will return the barrels they borrow in the September 29 exchange later with extra barrels. Bids are due October 6. Solicitations before the September 29 exchange had already awarded barrels, and deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="participating companies will return the 40 million borrowed barrels with additional premium barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="October 6, 2026" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="Deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December 2026" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6" data-absence>How much of the 100 million barrels is beyond the March pledge, and which countries will supply it, is unstated. The joint text leaves the split between diesel and crude unstated. On October 5 the White House said the release is 100 million barrels of refined diesel from Europe's strategic reserves within four months.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="produits raffinés, notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut, jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="In October 2026, President Trump successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves within the next four months." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Reuters reported that Brent settled down 6 cents, at $102.25 a barrel.<Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="Brent settled down 6 cents, or 0.06%, at $102.25 a barrel." /><Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="US oil prices dropped sharply - as much as 5% at one point" /></p>
          <p>The same story says WTI finished down $1.76, at $91.11 a barrel.<Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="WTI finished down $1.76, or 1.90%, at $91.11 a barrel." /></p>
          <p>France 24 quoted a remark that 80 percent of overall International Energy Agency stocks were “still in our pocket”. That is a share of all emergency stocks, not the share of March's 400 million barrels already released.<Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="80 percent of overall IEA stocks “still in our pocket”" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="blas-wholesale-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Javier Blas wrote that the price of US wholesale ultra-low sulphur diesel was back to the level of five weeks ago.<Cite s="blas-wholesale-2026-10-02" passage="The price of US wholesale ultra-low sulphur diesel is back to the level of five weeks ago." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Javier Blas, an energy and commodities columnist at Bloomberg<Cite s="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" passage="Energy and commodities columnist at Bloomberg." />, wrote on X<Cite s="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" passage="Javier Blas on X" /> that he sets the March question aside. Questions about whether the release includes March barrels that were not released, or were bought back, are a red herring, he wrote, because the question he treats as the important one is whether more barrels reach the market now.<Cite s="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" passage="Lots of questions about whether the G7 oil / diesel release includes barrels from March that were not released (and/or bought back). It's a red herring: the important question is whether more barrels reach the market now" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

